45417] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1034 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050322 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3fe45327-8fca-5b0c-bacd-05a7806eaeef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1031 "One-To-One Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1033 1886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050525 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5061be04-b0d3-5381-bdea-25cdacdaac9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](images/6f/6fbded3e0a3faefda0e1f2c8418f5e271fb116cde4082487096378f89511b695.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124283 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV  shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124284 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.   \n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1].$  Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\n          \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124285 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124472 #edb/ref 1038 #ed