 17592186396687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513202 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513203 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513209 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513221 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513226 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186513229 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186513242 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124474 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1045 "Invertible Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186124694 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7d6120fd-aa08-5562-9120-ce173e8fe311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1061 13908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b61f81e-5366-54d7-b4dc-eb12246fa126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125322 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1061 13909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ae142-fadd-53d9-975f-a485d1f363b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125323 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1061 13911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "067f6188-2d0b-5a28-8513-b138cb5eda82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1061 13916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "956e822f-5a25-5d3b-ac84-ada9c2c8dee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1064 "A function is **invertible** on an interval if and only if it's one-to-one. In other words, it must pass the horizontal line test.\n\nNot all functions have inverses! For example, consider the function $f(x)=x^2-1$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/e9/e99832fa1867a8314dda20f392d0c897ebb2942ac91a652ab1cf474b5d2d9612.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not a one-to-one since it does not satisfy the horizontal line test.\n\nTo find the graph of a function's inverse, we usually reflect it over the line $y=x.$ However, if we reflect $f(x)$ over the line $y=x,$ the result is not a function since it will not satisfy the vertical line test.\n\n![](images/7a/7a5a95f1cc3e2c8b40e865a24d22f4529e47c3c0137a6cb8d0369059f4a89a56.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is *not* invertible.\n\nIf a function is not invertible, we can sometimes make it invertible by restricting its domain.\n\nHere, if we take $f(x)=x^2-1$ over the restricted interval $x \\in (0,\\infty),$ then it passes the horizontal line test and we can find the corresponding inverse function by reflecting the graph of $y=f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/0a/0a7f98c2dce4599ee74cc13dc53c3a693b9c3716ffe54c708811f4c011f18b6d.png)\n\nTo determine if a function is invertible over a particular domain, we need to use the horizontal line test to check if it's a one-to-one function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1065 2768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90b8b9a7-2b60-5a13-9829-c02575ad8c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439130 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439131 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439132 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- **III.** $x\\in (2,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aef31e6b-2918-5842-b5c4-348781827064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323741 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-2|$ by $1$ unit up to get $f(x)=|x-2|+1.$\n\n![](images/ce/ceab1637313ae53869cc1e59f6a56ad6cb920a83dffba482558b696188df0d36.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (2,4].$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a4/a47169838785b6f4ac78253252bcedc3c2cb752a655feac9945e20dd40563b4f.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/14/14ed35294e1179ffd9efaf87fa8ac1eb99853a7da344e462c2cb0dda74c3b78f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439133 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439134 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439135 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n\n- **II.** $x\\in [-2,3]$\n\n- **III.** $x\\in [-3,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323742 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439224 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439225 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439226 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439227 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/3897b7fcf079b2522cbd0138631a894cbca1005cd241524cf9f3995965278612.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0655d813-1b1b-5f2a-9217-33c596819669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335886 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/95/95b1bd18e41cba7d6d1175f66be053ffea52a32fbb35e2ad1d126dbeb2d73baf.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I and III show one-to-one functions. This means that these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439232 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439233 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186439234 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,5)$\n\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26ae354c-e4a4-50d9-b820-8ba146650b10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186438202 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186438202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186438202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff35ccf9-01ac-52ac-880d-c4dc02e90a40" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186438203 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186438203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 175921