186439234 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d36afc1b-e6be-563c-8f1f-8a95a433bde4" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513056 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513080 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513088 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ef/efc2a0fcf4999a4ab32cdd5957a87ec7bf782cdb1ecac743551fd0509b983f6b.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nI. $x\\in (-2,0]$\n\nII. $x\\in [0,4]$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-1,4]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a972469-92c6-5025-b8f5-75835bec6279" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513032 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- On interval I, $x\\in (-2,0]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval II, $x\\in [0,4]$, the function is invertible. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the restricted graph at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- On interval III, $x\\in [-1,4]$, the function is **not** invertible. Some horizontal lines will intersect the restricted graph more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513071 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513074 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513086 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513034 #edb/ref 1071 "![](