ht; **III.** lower left; **IV.** lower right." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513180 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcef0170-a665-534f-b2df-558936bcb2e4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513180 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/1a/1a29e16b93980feb428e3386ee6fc95bc6afdce3d698f01a280303f6ff272597.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, II, IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, these graphs show one-to-one functions. As a result, these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graph III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513181 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186124472.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513181 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "23e39302-1f86-5aec-bd46-8b048883ad36" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513217 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513231 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513241 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n- **II.** $x\\in [0,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-2,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55cecc39-f460-5ecf-abe4-4ef195040d31" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513202 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe function is constant: distinct inputs in each listed interval have the same output $2$. Thus it is not one-to-one on any of the three intervals.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of these intervals\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513220 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513223 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513234 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [-1,0)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64c7ad19-8343-5b7b-a647-af679630a146" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513203 #edb/ref 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[-1,0)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $0\\le \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(0,1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{3}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{16}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513185 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513186 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513190 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513209 #edb/ref